---
import { siteUrl } from '../utils/site';
import Concept from '../layouts/Concept.astro';
import baseline from '../data/fidelity-grades.json';
import { scorecardIssuesUrl } from '../utils/scorecard';
const grades = baseline.areas;
---

<Concept title="Fidelity and quality baseline" section="Spec">
  <p><strong>October 8, 2026 · Owner playtest assessment.</strong> These grades preserve Kion’s current judgment. They mix authenticity, presentation and completeness; they are not measured percentages of original-game fidelity.</p>
  <p><strong>Provisional overall assessment: C+.</strong> This is an editorial summary, not an owner-assigned grade or a calculated average. Character flow and much of the presentation are strong; combat is C, while bosses, traps and systems remain D. Those core gaps matter more than a screen-count average.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Grade</th><th>Owner assessment</th><th>Next review target</th><th>Issues</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{grades.map(({area, grade, assessment, next, label}) => <tr><td>{area}</td><td><strong>{grade}</strong></td><td>{assessment}</td><td>{next}</td><td><a href={scorecardIssuesUrl(label)} aria-label={`Open issues for ${area}`}>Open issues ↗</a></td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>How grades change</h2>
  <p>A means convincing, faithful where intended, and polished with minor issues. B means good with visible gaps. C means the main loop works but important behavior or feel is incomplete. D means placeholder or rough implementation. Plus/minus refines the assessment; an unreviewed area stays ungraded.</p>
  <p>A grade increase SHOULD cite a dated user playtest, the revision, the scenario and the observed improvement. Automated passes MUST NOT independently promote a grade. Better-than-original presentation is a deliberate design choice and SHOULD be distinguished from original-game fidelity.</p>
  <h2>Combat evidence, tracked separately</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identity:</strong> reconcile runtime ID, display name, model, animation rig and source identity. <a href={siteUrl('/states/combat-roster')}>Current crosswalk and unresolved mappings</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Reference:</strong> cite original observation or decoded function/data, including revision and confidence. Clip names alone do not prove behavior.</li>
    <li><strong>Definition:</strong> align attack eligibility, phases, movement and forbidden behavior with the normative spec.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification:</strong> seeded tests plus real-rig probes, including misses, dodges, interruptions, walls and recovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Feel:</strong> focused user comparison before claiming a family is validated.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The existing suites provide substantial mechanics coverage. They do not establish every original attack, visual alignment or combat feel. Existing user approval of the gameplay foundation remains valid; family-level unknowns remain open.</p>
  <h2>Finding the next improvement</h2>
  <p>Each area links directly to open issues with its <code>score:*</code> label. An issue may improve several areas and carry several score labels. The scorecard owns the assessment and review targets; issues describe concrete defects, investigations or improvements with a clear completion condition.</p>
  <p>Use these filters to choose work without an umbrella issue or milestone per grade. Empty filters mean no open issues have been associated yet, not that the area is complete. Kion’s playtest observations can refine each review target independently of the issue list.</p>
</Concept>

<style>td { vertical-align: top; } table { width: 100%; } td:first-child { min-width: 110px; }</style>
